--TEST--
A cached entry is a copy in both directions — a write through the original does not reach it, each `get` answers a fresh one, and a value with no meaning across a copy boundary is refused — `rule:concurrency/a-cached-value-is-copied-across-the-boundary`
--FILE--
<?nvs
class Note {
    public string $text = "";
    public function constructor(string $text) {
        $this->text = $text;
    }
}

class Count {
    public static function oneTwo(): Iterator<int> {
        yield 1;
        yield 2;
    }
}

var $cache = Core\Cache::local();

// § 2: `put` copies *out* of the request heap. The request's own object stays
// the request's, so a write through it after the fact cannot reach the entry —
// which is what lets the entry outlive the heap that is dropped at the end of
// the request.
var $note = new Note("written");
$cache->put("note", $note);
$note->text = "changed after the put";
var $back = $cache->get("note") as Note;
echo "copy=", $back->text, "\n";

// § 2: and `get` copies *in*, once per call. Two reads are two graphs, so a
// write through the first is invisible to the second and no request can reach
// another's values by holding onto one.
$back->text = "changed after the get";
var $again = $cache->get("note") as Note;
echo "again=", $again->text, "\n";

// § 2: a cached value is subject to the same restrictions as any crossing
// value, because it *is* the same copy — so a closure, which captures a heap
// and a scope, is refused here exactly as it is at the isolate boundary.
var $fn = fn(): int => 1;
try {
    $cache->put("closure", $fn);
    echo "stored a closure\n";
} catch (LogicError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}

// A generator is refused the same way. Its state is a suspended frame, which
// has no meaning in another request.
try {
    $cache->put("generator", Count::oneTwo());
    echo "stored a generator\n";
} catch (LogicError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}

// The shared tier copies the same way, at the same boundary — the refusal
// below is the store's absence and not a second answer about what may cross.
try {
    Core\Cache::shared();
    echo "shared answered\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $unreachable) {
    echo "shared refused before there was anything to copy\n";
}
--EXPECT--
copy=written
again=written
Core\Cache\Store::put(): a closure captures a heap and a scope, so it has no meaning on the other side of a copy boundary
Core\Cache\Store::put(): a generator is a suspended frame, so it has no meaning on the other side of a copy boundary
shared refused before there was anything to copy
